WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.692
Welcome to Module 4 of the
Provider Notices learning path:

2
00:00:03.692 --> 00:00:06.575
Oracle Cloud Infrastructure announcements.

3
00:00:06.575 --> 00:00:10.174
This is Gate 3 of the provider
notices implementation guide,

4
00:00:10.174 --> 00:00:13.125
with the third chapter of
the developer workbook.

5
00:00:13.125 --> 00:00:16.948
Oracle Cloud Infrastructure
publishes maintenance, changes,

6
00:00:16.948 --> 00:00:21.800
action-required notices and production
events for a tenancy as announcements.

7
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.296
Updates to one announcement form a chain,

8
00:00:24.296 --> 00:00:28.700
and a rescheduled window arrives as
a new announcement in the same chain.

9
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:32.189
In this module you read the
fixture tenancy's announcements,

10
00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:36.654
see Oracle's own action-required date,
and watch the second week's rescheduled

11
00:00:36.654 --> 00:00:39.166
maintenance replace
the first in its chain,

12
00:00:39.166 --> 00:00:41.538
so it stays one notice
with the new window.

13
00:00:41.538 --> 00:00:43.860
It takes about forty-five minutes,

14
00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:47.176
with a lab on fixtures and
a lab for a test tenancy.

15
00:00:47.176 --> 00:00:53.170
The code is illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not run against a live tenancy.

16
00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.782
Why does Oracle Cloud
Infrastructure need its own gate?

17
00:00:59.782 --> 00:01:04.371
Oracle emails service status announcements
to the tenancy administrator.

18
00:01:04.371 --> 00:01:07.087
Anyone else receives them
only through managed

19
00:01:07.087 --> 00:01:10.008
email preferences or
announcement subscriptions.

20
00:01:10.008 --> 00:01:14.186
So an agency whose HR platform runs
on Oracle Cloud gets that platform's

21
00:01:14.186 --> 00:01:18.932
maintenance and change notices at whatever
address the tenancy was opened with.

22
00:01:18.932 --> 00:01:21.620
That might be the mailbox of
someone who has moved on.

23
00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:24.997
Reading the tenancy's
announcements here means platform

24
00:01:24.997 --> 00:01:28.445
maintenance reaches the HR
system's owners before it lands.

25
00:01:28.445 --> 00:01:30.733
And there's a wrinkle
the kit has to handle.

26
00:01:30.733 --> 00:01:33.170
Updates to one announcement form a chain.

27
00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.900
When Oracle reschedules
a maintenance window,

28
00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:40.031
the new time arrives as a new announcement
in the same chain, and a reader

29
00:01:40.031 --> 00:01:44.590
that keeps every announcement would
hold the old date next to the new one.

30
00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:51.253
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

31
00:01:51.253 --> 00:01:54.384
You'll write the one policy
statement the reader needs,

32
00:01:54.384 --> 00:01:57.377
and say what granting inspect
instead of read loses.

33
00:01:57.377 --> 00:02:01.075
You'll map Oracle's announcement
types to the record's kinds,

34
00:02:01.075 --> 00:02:03.490
with Oracle's own action-required date.

35
00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:07.753
You'll explain why the kit keeps only
the latest announcement in each chain.

36
00:02:07.753 --> 00:02:11.965
And you'll name the realms the kit
reads, and the one it refuses, and why.

37
00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:15.085
You need Module 1, for the
record and its action basis,

38
00:02:15.085 --> 00:02:19.728
and the Oracle Cloud column of the guide's
table comparing the five providers.

39
00:02:19.728 --> 00:02:23.890
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1
or later with Pester 5.

40
00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:25.310
No tenancy.

41
00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:31.310
Unit 1 is the guide's Step 3.

42
00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:33.696
The reader needs one policy statement,

43
00:02:33.696 --> 00:02:38.319
added by an I A M administrator to a
policy in the tenancy's root compartment:

44
00:02:38.319 --> 00:02:41.898
allow the reader's group to
read announcements in tenancy.

45
00:02:41.898 --> 00:02:45.589
Oracle's policy reference makes
announcements a resource type,

46
00:02:45.589 --> 00:02:48.097
and its own common policy
reads the same way.

47
00:02:48.097 --> 00:02:49.309
The verb matters.

48
00:02:49.309 --> 00:02:51.266
Inspect lists summaries only,

49
00:02:51.266 --> 00:02:55.335
without the affected resources
that the details call returns.

50
00:02:55.335 --> 00:02:59.773
With inspect, nothing can be routed
as naming the agency's resources.

51
00:02:59.773 --> 00:03:04.228
The kit's calls are Oracle's own: the
list command, for the whole tenancy,

52
00:03:04.228 --> 00:03:08.919
showing only the latest announcement
in each chain; then the get command,

53
00:03:08.919 --> 00:03:12.985
once per announcement, for its
details and affected resources.

54
00:03:12.985 --> 00:03:17.368
The command line is configured for
the reader with O C I setup config,

55
00:03:17.368 --> 00:03:22.350
in the tenancy's home region, and
Oracle's list command is the cross-check.

56
00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.275
Unit 2 reads the workbook chapter.

57
00:03:28.275 --> 00:03:31.101
Oracle has several
announcement types that mean

58
00:03:31.101 --> 00:03:33.375
the same kind of news,
so the kit maps them.

59
00:03:33.375 --> 00:03:36.275
A type that contains
maintenance is maintenance.

60
00:03:36.275 --> 00:03:40.043
A type that contains change,
or action required itself,

61
00:03:40.043 --> 00:03:44.062
is a change, and action required
is always Oracle's own flag.

62
00:03:44.062 --> 00:03:47.024
A production event
notification is an incident.

63
00:03:47.024 --> 00:03:50.400
Action recommended and
information are advisories.

64
00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.588
Oracle puts the window and the
deadline in two generic time fields,

65
00:03:54.588 --> 00:03:57.025
each with a type that says what it means.

66
00:03:57.025 --> 00:04:00.396
A start time, or a new start
time, starts the window;

67
00:04:00.396 --> 00:04:05.537
the end-time types end it; and action
required by is Oracle's own action date.

68
00:04:05.537 --> 00:04:08.226
So for Oracle Cloud,
no kit rule is needed:

69
00:04:08.226 --> 00:04:13.524
the provider says when action is required,
and the record's basis says provider.

70
00:04:13.524 --> 00:04:16.516
An inactive lifecycle
state closes the notice,

71
00:04:16.516 --> 00:04:21.240
and the source link is the announcement
on Oracle's own regional host.

72
00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:26.678
Two more things the kit adds.

73
00:04:26.678 --> 00:04:28.153
First, chains.

74
00:04:28.153 --> 00:04:32.290
The list call asks for only the
latest announcement in each chain,

75
00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:36.964
and the kit keeps only the latest by its
update time in each chain I D as well.

76
00:04:36.964 --> 00:04:40.949
The record's I D is the chain's, so
when Oracle reschedules a window,

77
00:04:40.949 --> 00:04:43.806
the notice keeps its I D
and gets the new window,

78
00:04:43.806 --> 00:04:47.715
and the router updates its task
instead of opening a second one.

79
00:04:47.715 --> 00:04:52.188
The command line's output key casing
isn't shown in Oracle's reference,

80
00:04:52.188 --> 00:04:57.053
so the kit accepts both styles; which one
the C L I prints is not yet specified.

81
00:04:57.053 --> 00:04:58.602
Second, realms.

82
00:04:58.602 --> 00:05:02.553
A government tenancy read in the
commercial realm returns nothing.

83
00:05:02.553 --> 00:05:07.095
The kit knows the commercial realm
and Oracle's U S Government Cloud,

84
00:05:07.095 --> 00:05:11.152
whose Announcements endpoints
are in U S Langley and U S Luke.

85
00:05:11.152 --> 00:05:14.021
As a side note, Oracle
lists that cloud with

86
00:05:14.021 --> 00:05:17.627
FedRAMP High and DISA Impact
Level 4 authorizations.

87
00:05:17.627 --> 00:05:22.886
The third realm is refused, because its
Announcements endpoint is not yet specified,

88
00:05:22.886 --> 00:05:26.030
and a region outside the
realm is refused too.

89
00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:30.505
Try it.

90
00:05:30.505 --> 00:05:35.561
Lab 1 runs in the provider notices
folder of the examples, in PowerShell 7,

91
00:05:35.561 --> 00:05:40.955
against a fictional tenancy in the
commercial realm, in the U S Ashburn region.

92
00:05:40.955 --> 00:05:42.743
Nothing signs in to anything.

93
00:05:42.743 --> 00:05:45.392
Step one lists what Oracle Cloud needs.

94
00:05:45.392 --> 00:05:46.380
Two calls.

95
00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:49.330
The list call needs only
inspect announcements.

96
00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:54.142
The get call, the details with their affected
resources, needs read announcements.

97
00:05:54.142 --> 00:05:55.626
So the policy grants read,

98
00:05:55.626 --> 00:05:59.581
because inspect alone would give
summaries without the resources.

99
00:05:59.581 --> 00:06:01.167
Both are marked verified.

100
00:06:01.167 --> 00:06:04.181
Step two asks for each
realm the kit knows.

101
00:06:04.181 --> 00:06:07.422
The commercial realm, on
oracle cloud dot com.

102
00:06:07.422 --> 00:06:12.242
The government realm, on oracle gov
cloud dot com, in its two regions.

103
00:06:12.242 --> 00:06:17.595
And the third realm is refused, with the
reason: its Announcements endpoint is

104
00:06:17.595 --> 00:06:23.380
not yet specified, because Oracle's page
wasn't readable on November ninth, 2026.

105
00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:31.591
Step three collects Oracle
Cloud from the day-one fixtures,

106
00:06:31.591 --> 00:06:36.780
as of November ninth, 2026: four
notices, three open, one needing action.

107
00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:40.817
One each of advisory, change,
incident and maintenance.

108
00:06:40.817 --> 00:06:42.818
Step four reads the records.

109
00:06:42.818 --> 00:06:46.253
Chain example one is
maintenance, starting November

110
00:06:46.253 --> 00:06:50.156
twenty-second at four in the morning,
U T C, on a database system.

111
00:06:50.156 --> 00:06:54.286
Because the details call named that
resource, Module 7's routing can

112
00:06:54.286 --> 00:06:58.343
ask for a contingency plan review
for the system that depends on it.

113
00:06:58.343 --> 00:07:03.605
Chain example two is a change, action
required by December fifteenth,

114
00:07:03.605 --> 00:07:08.129
2026, by Oracle's own action
required type, on a load balancer.

115
00:07:08.129 --> 00:07:10.617
That date is Oracle's, not the kit's.

116
00:07:10.617 --> 00:07:16.720
Chain example three is an advisory, and the
incident, chain example four, is closed.

117
00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:24.370
Step five collects the second
week, as of November sixteenth.

118
00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:25.908
Still four notices.

119
00:07:25.908 --> 00:07:30.596
But chain example one, the maintenance,
now runs on November twenty-ninth,

120
00:07:30.596 --> 00:07:32.940
from four to eight in the morning, U T C.

121
00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:38.020
Oracle rescheduled it, and the chain's
latest announcement replaced the first.

122
00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:42.000
It's one notice with the new
window, so Module 7 updates its task

123
00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.370
instead of opening a second one, and
nobody prepares for the old date.

124
00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:49.982
Step six builds the live list
command without running it.

125
00:07:49.982 --> 00:07:54.728
It's Oracle's own list command for
the fictional tenancy, with all pages,

126
00:07:54.728 --> 00:07:59.720
only the latest in each chain, the region
from the settings, and J SON output.

127
00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:02.207
A reviewer can see exactly what would run.

128
00:08:02.207 --> 00:08:06.733
Step seven runs this chapter's
tests: nine pass, none fail.

129
00:08:06.733 --> 00:08:08.940
Then delete the output folder.

130
00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:15.349
Lab 2 runs the same
read in an Oracle Cloud

131
00:08:15.349 --> 00:08:18.477
Infrastructure test
tenancy, never production.

132
00:08:18.477 --> 00:08:23.178
You need an I A M administrator, and
a test I A M group for the reader.

133
00:08:23.178 --> 00:08:26.170
The administrator adds
the read announcements in

134
00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:29.377
tenancy statement to a policy
in the root compartment.

135
00:08:29.377 --> 00:08:31.956
Then configure the command
line as the reader,

136
00:08:31.956 --> 00:08:35.891
and cross-check Oracle's own list
command against the kit's collection.

137
00:08:35.891 --> 00:08:38.211
Record one open item while you're there:

138
00:08:38.211 --> 00:08:42.853
the key casing the command line printed,
which Oracle's reference doesn't show.

139
00:08:42.853 --> 00:08:47.402
The kit accepts both, but the verification
log should say which one is real.

140
00:08:47.402 --> 00:08:51.082
To back it out, the I A M
administrator deletes the policy,

141
00:08:51.082 --> 00:08:55.241
and you delete the run, because
it names the tenancy's resources.

142
00:08:55.241 --> 00:08:57.390
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

143
00:08:57.390 --> 00:09:00.890
The workbook's Chapter 3
has the exact commands.

144
00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:07.252
The workbook chapter lists five dangers.

145
00:09:07.252 --> 00:09:09.587
If inspect is granted instead of read,

146
00:09:09.587 --> 00:09:13.050
the reader gets summaries
without affected resources,

147
00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:16.287
and nothing routes as naming
the agency's resources;

148
00:09:16.287 --> 00:09:20.353
an empty resources field on every
maintenance notice is the sign.

149
00:09:20.353 --> 00:09:24.281
If every announcement in a
chain is read as its own notice,

150
00:09:24.281 --> 00:09:29.715
the old window lingers next to the new one;
the kit keeps the latest in each chain.

151
00:09:29.715 --> 00:09:33.948
A government tenancy read in the
commercial realm returns nothing;

152
00:09:33.948 --> 00:09:37.803
the settings name the realm, and
a region outside it is refused.

153
00:09:37.803 --> 00:09:41.635
If the HR platform's maintenance
is seen only by the help desk,

154
00:09:41.635 --> 00:09:46.234
the HR system's owners learn of it after
it lands; the design guidance is to

155
00:09:46.234 --> 00:09:51.064
route Oracle maintenance that names the
HR system's resources to its owners.

156
00:09:51.064 --> 00:09:55.052
And the announcement email can be
spoofed and isn't in the evidence.

157
00:09:55.052 --> 00:09:58.343
It's the interim path only,
filed with the change record,

158
00:09:58.343 --> 00:10:02.300
with the recipients named in
the settings' notice contacts.

159
00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:07.437
The federal requirement.

160
00:10:07.437 --> 00:10:12.317
Gate 3 supports NIST special
publication eight hundred

161
00:10:12.317 --> 00:10:15.950
fifty-three S A nine, part
c, and S I five, part a.

162
00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:20.976
And when maintenance names the agency's
resources, it supports C P two, part e:

163
00:10:20.976 --> 00:10:24.057
updating the contingency
plan for changes to

164
00:10:24.057 --> 00:10:26.975
the system or its
environment of operation.

165
00:10:26.975 --> 00:10:30.787
The evidence is the permission
list and the policy statement;

166
00:10:30.787 --> 00:10:35.121
the raw Oracle response, with the
list and each announcement's details,

167
00:10:35.121 --> 00:10:36.915
the notices and the manifest,

168
00:10:36.915 --> 00:10:40.726
every chain's latest announcement
read in the tenancy's realm;

169
00:10:40.726 --> 00:10:43.641
maintenance that names
the agency's resources,

170
00:10:43.641 --> 00:10:47.079
so Module 7's routing asks
for a contingency plan review;

171
00:10:47.079 --> 00:10:51.638
and a rescheduled window that replaces
the original and stays one notice.

172
00:10:51.638 --> 00:10:54.450
The gate's output is
evidence for these controls;

173
00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:58.738
your organization still sets their
parameters, and the assessor decides.

174
00:10:58.738 --> 00:11:02.774
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers,

175
00:11:02.774 --> 00:11:07.090
and which file supports which
indicator is the program's mapping.

176
00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:12.815
Check yourself with two questions.

177
00:11:12.815 --> 00:11:16.377
First: what does granting
inspect instead of read lose?

178
00:11:16.377 --> 00:11:17.603
The answer is b.

179
00:11:17.603 --> 00:11:20.052
The details call's affected resources.

180
00:11:20.052 --> 00:11:24.635
Inspect lists summaries only, so
no notice can be routed as naming

181
00:11:24.635 --> 00:11:28.964
the agency's resources, and no
contingency review is asked for.

182
00:11:28.964 --> 00:11:32.253
Second: Oracle reschedules
a maintenance window.

183
00:11:32.253 --> 00:11:34.652
How many notices does the kit hold for it?

184
00:11:34.652 --> 00:11:40.091
The answer is b, one: the latest announcement
in the chain, with the new window.

185
00:11:40.091 --> 00:11:44.902
The record's I D is the chain's, so
the task is updated, not doubled.

186
00:11:44.902 --> 00:11:47.329
The module page has three more questions,

187
00:11:47.329 --> 00:11:50.190
each linked to the
section that answers it.

188
00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:54.765
To sum up.

189
00:11:54.765 --> 00:11:57.208
You read the reader's
one policy statement,

190
00:11:57.208 --> 00:12:00.070
saw the realms the kit reads
and the one it refuses,

191
00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:03.560
collected four announcements
with Oracle's own action date,

192
00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:07.190
and watched a rescheduled
maintenance window stay one notice.

193
00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:11.828
The evidence an ISSO keeps for
Gate 3 is the raw Oracle response,

194
00:12:11.828 --> 00:12:17.270
with the list and each announcement's
details, the notices as JSON and as

195
00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:22.265
a spreadsheet file, and the manifest,
all dated; and in Lab 2, the policy.

196
00:12:22.265 --> 00:12:26.201
For a system that runs on Oracle
Cloud, such as an HR platform,

197
00:12:26.201 --> 00:12:31.503
route maintenance that names its resources
to its owners, which Module 7 builds.

198
00:12:31.503 --> 00:12:34.577
Before you ship, work through
the chapter's checklist.

199
00:12:34.577 --> 00:12:38.878
Next is Module 5: AWS Health
and the support plan.

200
00:12:38.878 --> 00:12:42.223
That's Gate 4, with the
workbook's fourth chapter:

201
00:12:42.223 --> 00:12:45.900
three read actions, and a
support plan the A P I needs.
